(a) The mayor shall have the right to declare an emergency to exist when, in his opinion, any one or more of the following conditions exist:
(1) There is extreme likelihood of danger of destruction of life or property due to unusual conditions;
(2) Unusual or extreme weather conditions make the use of city streets or other areas difficult or impossible;
(3) Civil unrest, commotion or uprising is imminent or exists; or
(4) There is a stoppage or loss of electrical power affecting a major portion of the city.
(b) After the declaration of such emergency the mayor, as chief executive, may exercise any or all of the following powers:
(1) The power to use employees of the city to assist in the safety and preservation of life, limb and property of the citizenry of the city;
(2) The power to close streets and sidewalks and to delineate areas within the city wherein an emergency exists;
(3) The power to impose emergency curfew regulations;
(4) The power to close business establishments within the affected area;
(5) The power to close any and all city-owned buildings and other facilities to the use of the general public; or
(6) The power to do any and all acts necessary and incidental to the preservation of life, limb, and property of the citizenry of the city.
(c) No emergency power set forth in this section may be effective for more than 72 hours after the declaration of an emergency by the mayor. However, upon the declaration of a second or further emergencies to exist, the emergency powers set forth in this section may be exercised during such further emergency period or periods, but never for more than 72 hours in one declared emergency period.
(d) No emergency period shall extend beyond the next regular or special meeting of the council.
(e) The declaration of an emergency as to a portion of the city shall specify with exactness the area in which the emergency is declared to exist.
(Code 1986, § 2-18)
Charter reference— Extra policemen in case of emergency, § 20.

